  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. x is the steep setting, so overshoot shows across the screen first. Without noise, steps diverge for learning rates above 2/3.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per component has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 2 times their true length, one scale for both: the mini-batch step the ball will take, and the step the full 64-point data would give. Steps go against the slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss exceeds 8, the highest loss on the drawn bowl (diverged). Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seeded sequence; New run continues the sequence and keeps the last path as a ghost. Reset clears both and replays the sequence from the start."
  ]
}